(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-abdominal-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Abdominal Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The bladder is generally examinable on physical exam only when...","choices":["never, regardless of its degree of fullness","only while the patient remains standing upright","it remains completely empty and non-distended","it is distended above the symphysis pubis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The bladder is usually not palpable/percussible unless it is distended, at which point it rises above the symphysis pubis and can be assessed for a round, smooth dome, tenderness, and the height it reaches (via percussion for dullness). — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"A distended bladder, when examined, should have a dome that feels...","choices":["irregular and nodular","stony-hard","it cannot be assessed by touch at all","round and smooth"],"answer":3,"exp":"A distended bladder's dome should feel round and smooth on palpation; the examiner also checks for tenderness and percusses to assess how high it rises above the symphysis pubis (as an area of dullness). — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"To assess the aortic pulsation, the examiner presses firmly deep in the upper abdomen...","choices":["far laterally in the right upper quadrant","only within the pelvic cavity itself","only directly along the costal margins","slightly to the left of the midline"],"answer":3,"exp":"The aorta is identified by pressing firmly and deeply in the upper abdomen, slightly to the left of the midline, where its pulsation can typically be felt. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"In patients over 50 years old, assessing aortic width by pressing deeply with one hand on each side of the aorta, a normal aortic width should not exceed approximately...","choices":["0.5 cm","there is no defined normal width","3.0 cm","10.0 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"In patients over 50, aortic width is specifically assessed because of increased risk of aneurysm with age; a normal aorta should measure no more than about 3.0 cm wide, with wider measurements raising concern for an abdominal aortic aneurysm. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"To distinguish an abdominal wall mass from a true intra-abdominal mass, the examiner asks the patient to raise their head/shoulders or do a partial sit-up (tensing the abdominal muscles). An abdominal wall mass will...","choices":["become impossible to feel once the muscles are tensed","disappear entirely with no further significance","move to a completely different quadrant","remain palpable despite the tensed abdominal muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because an abdominal wall mass sits within or on the muscle layer itself, it remains palpable even when the abdominal muscles are tensed (for example, during a partial sit-up); a true intra-abdominal mass, by contrast, becomes harder to feel once the tensed muscles are interposed between it and the examiner's hand. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 56"},{"q":"Ensuring the patient has an empty bladder before the abdominal exam is a recommended tip primarily because a full bladder can...","choices":["have no meaningful effect on the exam's accuracy or the patient's comfort","eliminate the examiner's ongoing need to perform percussion elsewhere in the abdomen","cause discomfort and be mistaken for or obscure other pelvic/abdominal findings","meaningfully improve the accuracy of liver span assessment obtained by percussion"],"answer":2,"exp":"A full bladder can cause patient discomfort during palpation and can be mistaken for (or make it harder to assess) other pelvic or lower abdominal findings, which is why ensuring an empty bladder beforehand is recommended. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"The tip 'if pain, know where the pain is — exam that area last' reflects the same principle as...","choices":["examining the painful area first, ahead of every other region","skipping the painful area from the examination entirely and permanently","examining any area of known/suspected tenderness last during palpation","using only auscultation, never palpation, on painful areas"],"answer":2,"exp":"This tip reflects the broader palpation principle of leaving the tender area for last, to avoid inducing guarding that would make it harder to accurately assess the rest of the abdomen. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Warming your hands and stethoscope before the abdominal exam (a noted tip) primarily helps to...","choices":["meaningfully improve the acoustic quality of percussion notes","eliminate the ongoing need to ask about pain verbally","fully sterilize the equipment before each patient use","reduce involuntary muscle guarding triggered by a cold touch"],"answer":3,"exp":"A cold touch can cause the patient to involuntarily tense their abdominal muscles (guarding), which interferes with accurate palpation; warming hands and the stethoscope beforehand helps minimize this reflexive response. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Watching the patient's face throughout the abdominal exam (a noted tip) is recommended mainly to...","choices":["gauge discomfort or pain in real time, even if the patient doesn't verbalize it","formally assess the patient's visual acuity and pupillary response","fully replace the need to ever ask the patient about pain verbally","check exclusively for signs of jaundice in the sclera and skin tone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Facial expressions can reveal discomfort or pain the patient may not immediately verbalize, giving the examiner real-time feedback to adjust technique or check in directly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"For proper positioning during abdominal inspection, the examiner should...","choices":["stand only at the foot of the bed, without approaching either side of the patient","stand at the right side of the bed and sit or bend to view the abdomen tangentially","view the abdomen only from directly above while standing fully upright at all times","stand exclusively on the patient's left side throughout the entire abdominal exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"The examiner stands at the patient's right side and sits or bends down to view the abdomen tangentially (at eye level with the abdominal surface), which helps detect subtle contour changes, bulges, or visible peristalsis/pulsations that might be missed from a higher viewing angle. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"During abdominal inspection, the examiner systematically evaluates the skin, umbilicus, scars, striae, dilated veins, and...","choices":["only the frequency, pitch, and character of auscultated bowel sounds","only CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness elicited by fist percussion","rashes/lesions, contour, location of findings, inflammation, and bulges","only the measured span of the liver obtained by percussion technique"],"answer":2,"exp":"The systematic inspection checklist includes the skin, umbilicus, scars, striae, dilated veins, rashes and lesions, overall contour, the location of any findings, signs of inflammation, and any visible bulges. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"When assessing abdominal contour on inspection, the examiner should note whether it is...","choices":["flat, round, protuberant, or scaphoid, along with any symmetry or bulging areas","only its surface temperature as felt with the back of the examiner's hand","contour is never formally assessed as part of the standard inspection step","only whether the abdomen is tender or non-tender to gentle palpation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The contour assessment specifically screens for whether the abdomen is flat, round, protuberant (distended), or scaphoid (concave, sunken), plus checking overall symmetry and any localized bulging areas. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Visible peristalsis on abdominal inspection, which may require observing for several minutes (about one minute per quadrant), can be a normal finding in...","choices":["thin patients, though it can also indicate obstruction in other contexts","it is assessed only by deep palpation, never by visual inspection","never a normal finding, always signifying bowel obstruction","only in patients with obesity, due to a thickened abdominal wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Visible peristaltic waves can be a normal finding in thin patients with little abdominal fat overlying the bowel wall, but in the appropriate clinical context, prominent visible peristalsis can also indicate bowel obstruction, which is why patient observation over time can be informative. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"A spider angioma noted on abdominal skin inspection is a vascular skin finding that can be associated with...","choices":["a completely normal finding with no clinical association","chronic liver disease","acute appendicitis","normal aging with no other significance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Spider angiomas (small, central arterioles with radiating thin vessels resembling a spider) are a recognized cutaneous sign associated with chronic liver disease, related to altered estrogen metabolism in that condition. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"After capturing the kidney between both hands at peak inspiration, the examiner then asks the patient to breathe out and briefly stop breathing while...","choices":["moving directly to fist percussion over the costovertebral angle region","repeating the deep breath immediately without ever releasing the hands","slowly releasing the right hand to feel the kidney slide back into position","immediately and abruptly releasing both hands at the exact same moment"],"answer":2,"exp":"As the patient exhales and briefly pauses, the examiner slowly releases the right hand, feeling for the sensation of the kidney sliding back into its normal position between the hands — part of the complete bimanual capture technique. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"To examine the left kidney using the bimanual capture technique, the examiner moves to the patient's left side and...","choices":["uses the identical hand positioning used for the right kidney","switches to fist percussion instead of palpation on the left","does not attempt to capture or palpate the left kidney at all","uses the opposite hand positioning from the right-sided technique"],"answer":3,"exp":"Examining the left kidney requires moving to the patient's left side and mirroring the hand positions used for the right kidney (opposite hand placement), repeating the same capture maneuver. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"During bimanual liver palpation, the examiner's right hand is initially placed on the middle of the right abdomen with fingers positioned...","choices":["together, pointing toward the patient's head","spread apart, pointing toward the patient's feet","positioned only on the left side of the abdomen","in a fist, not extended"],"answer":0,"exp":"The right hand is placed with fingers together, pointing cephalad (toward the patient's head), which orients the fingers to catch the liver edge as it descends with inspiration. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"If the liver edge is felt during palpation, the examiner should gently roll the hand over it to assess for...","choices":["hearing acuity and vestibular balance","auscultated lung sounds bilaterally","systemic blood pressure readings","tenderness, enlargement, or masses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Once the liver edge is felt, gently rolling the palpating hand over it allows assessment of tenderness, enlargement (hepatomegaly), or any palpable masses along the edge. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"Bruits, if present over the aorta or renal/iliac/femoral arteries, are best described as...","choices":["best detected using the stethoscope bell pressed lightly against the abdominal skin","abnormal vascular sounds suggesting turbulent blood flow, often from narrowing (stenosis)","a completely normal finding expected to be heard in every healthy adult patient","identical in mechanism and clinical origin to bowel-derived borborygmi sounds"],"answer":1,"exp":"A bruit is an abnormal whooshing/blowing vascular sound caused by turbulent blood flow, often due to narrowing (stenosis) of the vessel being auscultated, and is a distinctly different finding from bowel sounds. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"Ensuring the patient is comfortable, with appropriate arm and gown positioning, before beginning the abdominal exam (noted tips) primarily supports...","choices":["nothing relevant to the accuracy of the resulting exam findings","improved percussion note volume only, with no other benefit","faster completion of the exam only, entirely without accuracy benefit","relaxed abdominal musculature, which allows for a more accurate exam"],"answer":3,"exp":"A comfortable, well-positioned patient (proper arm position, appropriately arranged gown) is more likely to have relaxed abdominal muscles, which is essential for accurate palpation and percussion — a tense, uncomfortable patient tends to guard, obscuring findings. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Dilated abdominal wall veins noted on inspection, especially when radiating from the umbilicus (caput medusae), most classically reflect...","choices":["acute appendicitis presenting with localized right lower quadrant inflammation and peritoneal irritation","normal aging changes only, entirely unrelated to any underlying hepatic disease or vascular pathology","portal hypertension, often from cirrhosis, causing collateral venous flow through the umbilical/periumbilical veins","a completely normal finding present in all healthy adults regardless of liver or vascular status"],"answer":2,"exp":"Caput medusae (dilated veins radiating from the umbilicus) classically reflects portal hypertension, often due to cirrhosis, where blood is rerouted through recanalized umbilical/periumbilical collateral veins. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Keeping fingernails trimmed short (a noted practical tip, 'no long nails') is important for the abdominal exam mainly to...","choices":["avoid accidentally scratching or causing discomfort to the patient during palpation","meaningfully improve the acoustic quality of the percussion notes obtained","have no real relevance whatsoever to patient comfort or overall exam technique","fully sterilize the examiner's hands before beginning the abdominal exam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Long fingernails risk inadvertently scratching or poking the patient during palpation, causing unnecessary discomfort; trimmed nails allow for gentler, more controlled technique. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"An abdominal exam that skips auscultation and moves directly from inspection to percussion/palpation risks...","choices":["improving the accuracy of the subsequent percussion findings obtained","no meaningful consequence at all for the patient or the exam","obtaining a misleadingly altered assessment of bowel sound activity","making the exam faster overall, with no meaningful downsides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Percussion and palpation can mechanically stimulate the bowel, artificially increasing peristaltic activity; skipping auscultation first (or performing it out of order) risks an inaccurate assessment of the patient's true baseline bowel sound activity. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"Percussion of the abdomen serves several purposes, including assessing for gas, mass, and...","choices":["blood pressure","hearing thresholds","organomegaly","visual acuity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Percussion helps assess for gas (tympany), masses, and organomegaly (enlarged organs), using the pattern of tympany versus dullness to characterize underlying structures. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"The rationale for performing light palpation before deep palpation is that light palpation...","choices":["is performed only after deep palpation has already been fully completed, never beforehand","identifies superficial tenderness/guarding first, informing how to safely proceed with deeper palpation","completely and permanently replaces the need to ever perform deep palpation afterward","has absolutely no meaningful bearing on how or where deep palpation is subsequently performed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Light palpation first identifies areas of superficial tenderness or muscular guarding, which helps the examiner anticipate and safely approach subsequent deep palpation, particularly around any sensitive areas. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"A positive splenic percussion sign, where the percussion note changes to dullness during inspiration in the left anterior axillary line's lowest intercostal space, suggests...","choices":["a completely normal spleen","liver disease exclusively, unrelated to the spleen","splenic enlargement (splenomegaly)","kidney enlargement"],"answer":2,"exp":"A positive splenic percussion sign — where a change from tympany to dullness occurs with inspiration in this location — suggests that the spleen has enlarged enough to be percussible in that area, that is, splenomegaly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Noting the specific location of any inspected finding (for example, a scar, bulge, or lesion) on the abdomen is important because location can help...","choices":["completely replace the need for subsequent palpation of that same area","only matter for findings related specifically to the cardiac auscultation exam","become entirely irrelevant to documentation once a finding has already been noted","narrow the differential and correlate the finding with underlying anatomic structures"],"answer":3,"exp":"The abdomen's regions correspond to different underlying organs; documenting the precise location of a finding helps correlate it with likely underlying anatomic structures and narrows the differential diagnosis. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Combining the hooking technique with the standard bimanual approach for liver palpation is useful because...","choices":["these particular combined techniques are used exclusively for the spleen, never applied to the liver at all","the hooking technique entirely and permanently replaces the ongoing need for abdominal auscultation","different techniques can be more sensitive for different body habitus/liver positions, improving overall detection","only one single technique is ever truly needed in practice, and the other is never actually used"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because liver position and patient body habitus vary, having multiple techniques (standard bimanual and hooking) available increases the overall sensitivity of the exam for detecting the liver edge across different patients. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"If a patient is noted to be particularly ticklish during the abdominal exam (a practical consideration mentioned in lecture), the examiner should...","choices":["avoid palpating the abdomen entirely for the remainder of the visit","speed through the entire exam as quickly as physically possible","politely ask the patient to step out of the exam room immediately","use firm, confident, steady pressure rather than light, tentative touch"],"answer":3,"exp":"A light, tentative touch tends to provoke a ticklish response more than firm, confident, steady pressure; using firmer technique from the start helps minimize this reaction and allows a more effective exam. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"Overall, the specialized maneuvers for spleen, kidney, bladder, and aorta assessment share the common purpose of...","choices":["completely and permanently replacing the need for the remainder of the standard abdominal exam","assessing only surface skin color and pigmentation changes across the entire abdominal wall","detecting organ enlargement, abnormal masses, or vascular abnormalities not evident on simpler inspection alone","providing no meaningful diagnostic value whatsoever beyond simple visual inspection alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"These specialized percussion/palpation maneuvers (splenic percussion sign, kidney capture technique, bladder percussion, aortic width assessment) all serve to detect organ enlargement, masses, or vascular abnormalities that would not be apparent from inspection alone, adding diagnostic depth to the abdominal exam. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 45"}]};
